the D. dim. suffix ''-je'', which is short for ''-jen'', a later var. of ''-ken'' (see ''-kin'').] A diminutive suffix, appearing chiefly in childish names of animals, etc., as ''kitty'', ''doggy'', ''piggy'', ''birdy'', ''froggy'', ''mousy'', and similar names, or familiar forms of personal names, as ''Katy'' or ''Kitty'' (diminutive of ''Kate''), ''Jenny'', ''Hetty'', ''Fanny'', ''Willy'', ''Johnny'', ''Tommy'', etc., such names being often spelled with ''-ie'', as ''Willie'', ''Davie'', etc., a spelling common in Scotch use, and also in general use in names of girls, as ''Katie'', ''Jennie'', ''Hettie'', ''Carrie'', ''Lizzie'', ''Nellie'', ''Annie'', etc. {{smaller|Such names coincide in terminal form with some feminine names not actually diminutive, as ''Mary'', ''Lucy'', ''Lily'', formerly and sometimes still written ''Marie'', ''Lucie'', ''Lillie'', etc. The diminutive termination is not used, except as above, in English literary speech, but it is common in Scotch, as in ''beastie'', ''mannie'', ''lassie'', sometimes with a second diminutive suffix, as in ''lassiekie'', etc.}}

